<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cunobelinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 40 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Cymbeline</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cunobelinus was a king of the Catuvellauni who ruled much of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from about 9 to 40 CE from his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a>. He issued many gold coins with an ear of grain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> calls him king of the Britons. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togodumnus">Togodumnus</a> led resistance to the Roman invasion of 43, and he is the original of Shakespeare's Cymbeline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cunobelinus.jpg" alt="Cunobelinus" width="320" height="595" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cunobelin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For the next century Rome dealt with British rulers through diplomacy, and southern kings such as <b>Cunobelinus</b> of the Catuvellauni struck coins and imported wine and fine pottery from the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressure from the Catuvellauni under <b>Cunobelinus</b> and his sons steadily shrank the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates-britain">Atrebates (Britain)</a></li><li><blockquote>In the decades before the Claudian invasion, coins of Dubnovellaunus and later the influence of <b>Cunobelinus</b> of the Catuvellauni suggest Kent fell under the sway of more powerful northern neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/cantiaci">Cantiaci</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cunobelinus</b>, ruling from about 9 to 40 CE from Camulodunum, which he took from the Trinovantes, extended influence over Kent and much of the south-east; Suetonius called him king of the Britons.</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 10 CE the Catuvellaunian king <b>Cunobelinus</b> took Camulodunum and made it his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinovantes">Trinovantes</a></li></ul></section>
